%n ana šūši | bērā || nummât | qištum
#note: See @bib{RN117@209}: “For @i{bērā} see the study of the construction and writing of metrological units in the distributive by @bib{powell1982adverbian}. As noted by @bib{RN2652@113}, the sign before danna is not IGI-@i{gunû} (i.e., 10,000) but an abbreviated ligature of @i{šu-ši}. The SB text has 60-@i{šu bēr} and would hardly be guilty of a revision downwards. The penultimate word has been read in several different ways: Jastrow and Clay proposed @i{nu-ma-at}, which Langdon connected @i{nu-ma-at} with ‘Arabic @i{nawa}’ (@bib{langdon1929notes@346}). Thompson read @i{bat-ba-at} < @i{battubattu}, though this word is not found outside Assyria. Then Landsberger proposed @i{til-ma-at}, ‘ist umgeben’ (loc. cit.), which he derived from the root √@i{lwʾ}, supposing an exceptional metathesis @i{tilmât} < @i{litwât}. In support of this decipherment he cited @akk{RI-ma-at} in SB II 293, which he read as @i{tal-ma-at} (‘Überlieferungsfehler für @i{tilmât} oder Plural von @i{talwītu}’). However, now that the SB word is securely deciphered as @i{rimmat}, after the parallels elsewhere in SB II (223 and 280), and now that Assyrian MS y₂ obv. 13’ supplies another word again (@i{lamâssu}), the reading @i{til-ma-at} has lost all foundation and looks very implausible on orthographic and lexical grounds (see already C. Wilcke, RLA IV, p. 533). The way out of the difficulty is to accept that the OB word is not related to its later counterparts and to assume that later editors, to whom @i{nu-ma-at} was obscure, replaced it and otherwise altered the line –in SB II by interpolating @i{išemmēma}— to produce a satisfactory meaning. Schott’s original comment that @i{nu-ma-at} must be a stative governed by @i{qištum} holds good (@bib{schott1934zu@108}), and the only possible verb in our present knowledge of Akkadian is @i{nawûm}. Though often translated as ‘to lie in ruins, to be laid waste’, the sense of this verb seems to be as much to do with a lack of human presence as to do with ruined buildings (cf. the cognate noun @i{nawûm}, which refers to the uninhabited regions beyond the arable land, the great tracts of largely empty country where sheep were pastured). The absence of human occupation is a conception which suits every occurrence and exactly fits the inaccessible and unvisited Cedar Forest (cf. von Soden, ‘liegt unberührt’: Reclam⁴ ⁵). Consequently I take the verb to mean ‘to be(come) a desolation, wilderness'. The stative of the II/1 adjective will be an elative of the simple stem. The fact that we have @i{nummât} rather than @i{nuwwât} is no objection in the light of the existence in good OB contexts of comparable attestations of the cognate noun (@i{MSL} XII, p. 158, OB @i{Lu} A 263: @i{na-me-e}; XIII, p. 229, @i{Kagal} I ii 62: @akk{na-mu-u₂}, late OB). The three couplets that begin with this line are repeated as ll. 195-200.” Attia (@bib{NABU2005-77}) suggested understanding the signs as @akk{NU ma-at}, “c’est plus le Pays, c’est la Forêt.” See @bib{RN2651@10b}: “Recently Attia (2005) proposed @akk{NU ma-at}, “c’est plus le Pays, c’est la Forêt.” The result is not far from the author’s translation, but epigraphically it is not possible to accept this suggestion. First, nowhere in the OB epic is @i{lā} written with @akk{NU}. Secondly, the suggested double nominal phrase @i{lā māt qištum}, though theoretically not impossible, is not convincing. The typical way to construct nominal phrases in the epic is found in iii 110-112, and in iii 132. In addition, @i{mātum} is a neutral term for any land, not necessarily for a civilized one (which would probably be dadmii). Hence, @i{lā māt} is “ce n’est pas le pays,” and not with capital “c’est plus le Pays.” Further, the sentence begins with a prepositional phrase that logically leads to what was “for sixty leagues in each direction …,” not to what was not. And finally, the Cedar Forest is not found on the fringes of civilization, near the city of Uruk and Sumer in general, but miles away from the comrades’ home. Thus, even if @i{mātum} could designate “civilization,” the heroes have left the civilized world much earlier.”
// L I.4 OB "III" 195
// L I.4 SB "II" 223
// L I.4 SB "II" 293
