%n pulhiātim | sebe || iš[īmšum | ellil?]
#note: See @bib{RN117@211}: “The later texts have the line as @i{ana pulḫāti} (y @i{pulḫēte}) @i{ša nišī išīmšu Ellil} (Assyrian MS y₂ obv. 15'; SB II 219b // 228 // 285 // 299). Though others have thought differently–Tigay reads @akk{i-[din-šu {d}en-lil?]} (@bib{RN809@283}), Tournay and Shaffer read @akk{it-[ta-al-bi-iš]} (@bib{RN580@85, fn. 40})–I assume that the OB line ends in the same way. With regard to the first word, von Soden noted (@bib{LS304@213}) that the value @i{ḫá} for @akk{HI.A} is not found in Akkadian context but proposed the reading @i{pu-ul-ḫá-tim} nevertheless; the value has found its way into his syllabary (@bib{AkkSyll@47}). This strategy is unnecessary, however, for ‘broken’ fem. pl. nouns are an occasional feature of Akkadian (I owe this observation and the following references to W. G. Lambert): OB @akk{i-na um-me-a-tim} (@i{ummātim}) @akk{um-šum ib-ba-aš-ši}, ‘in summer there will be a heatwave’ (@i{YOS} X 22, 22); MB @akk{be-el ma-ti-a-ti} (@i{mātāti}), ‘lord of the lands’ (@i{BE} I 41+46, 2; Kurigalzu); LB @akk{a-ba-aš-lam i-na gi-ninda₂-na-kum u₂-ma-an-di-da mi-in-di-a-tam} (@i{mindāti}), ‘the surveyor measured the dimensions with a twelve-cubit rod’ (@i{BE} I 84 ii 25-7, cf. 38 @i{mi-in-di-a-tim}; Nbp). Note also, in this book, a ‘broken’ dual, @i{bir-ki-a-šú} for @i{birkāšu} in a LB manuscript (SB I 200, MS n).” For more examples of “broken” fem. pl., see @bib{NABU2004-49} and @bib{RN3043@206 fn. 2}.
