%n gilgāmeš | itti huwāwa (|) dapīnim || taqumtam | ištu
#note: See @bib{RN117@211}: “I agree with W. G. Lambert that the first sign is not @i{tu} (@bib{RN416@271}, from the unpublished copy of Shaffer) but to my eyes it is @i{ta} rather than @i{iš}. The spelling @i{ta-qum-tam} is meant for @i{tuqumtam}, as anticipated by @bib{vonsoden1955boehl@514}, and newly confirmed by the Assyrian MS y₁ 5’, which has the logogram @akk{GIŠ.LA₂}. A similar syllabic spelling is also known from an OB school textbook from Nippur (@bib{RN1308@4, 14′}: @akk{{ki-še-eš-bi}|UR×UR|} = @akk{a-šar ta-qum-tim#}); the existence of two examples of this form of the word implies lexical variation of the kind exhibited by the pair @i{tarbuʾtum} : @i{turbuʾtum}. The expression @i{tuqumtam šatûm} is a vividly figurative idiom, literally ‘to weave battle’, that likens the parry and thrust of man-to-man combat to the interlacing of warp and weft on the loom. It occurs in other texts: in an OB legend about Narām-Sîn (@bib{temp_id_8614570978802014@191, i 1}; also @bib{RN262@278}): @akk{qa₂-ab-lam iš-ta-tu i-na mu-uh-hi-ša#}, ‘he joined battle against her (@i{or} it)’; in Bullussa-rabi’s hymn to Gula (@bib{RN775@122, 111}): @i{šá-tu-ú a-na-an-tum}, ‘(Ninurta), who joins combat’; and in a late hymn to Ninurta (@bib{RN358@21, 2a}): @akk{[i]-na qe₂-reb te-še-e a-šar ša₂-ta-at a-nun-tu}, ‘in the midst of havoc, where combat is joined'. The image is elaborated in a bilingual litany which celebrates the goddess Inanna’s role in combat (@bib{RN1719@200}, @i{Uruammairrabi} XXI 72): @sux{me₃.a gu mu.dun.dun} // @akk{ina ta-ha-zi qe₂-e a-šat-tu₄}, “in battle I interweave the threads”.”
// L I.4 NA "y" 5'

