#note: See @bib{RN117@214–215}: “255–6. The couplet is remarkable for the tense of its verbs. In l. 256 there is hardly room to restore @akk{i-[na-ṣa-ar]} and @i{ušallim} in l. 255 is certainly preterite. Some have ignored the tense, while others have seen an allusion to some otherwise unknown episode in which Enkidu saved a companion earlier in his career. The key, however, is the phenomenon recently discussed by @bib{RN2725}. Mayer observed that in texts such as the Šamaš Hymn and Marduk Prayer No. 2 the preterite tense is found alongside the present and stative in verbs that refer to the typical: ‘Bei Aussagen über typische Situationen, Geschehnisse, Handlungen und Verhaltensweisen finden wir hier nämlich nebeneinander den Stativ, das Präsens-und das Präteritum, letzeres manchmal sogar in Parallelismus mit einem Präsens’ (p. 392). The parallelism he notes is exactly that occurring between @i{ušezzeb} and @i{iṣṣur} in the late version of this couplet (SB III 4-5: @i{ālik maḫri tappâ ušezzeb} / @i{ša ṭūdu īdû ibiršu iṣṣur}, cf. SB III 218-19). Mayer expected to find the ‘gnomic preterite’ in Akkadian proverbs and everyday expressions but noted just one example. I am inclined to think that this couplet contains two more (Kovacs took the same view of the late equivalent of the couplet). Other apparently proverbial sayings in SB Gilgameš using the ‘gnomic preterite’ are to be found in SB VII 75-6: @i{ana balṭi} […] @i{īzibū nasāsa} / [@i{mī}]@i{tu}(?) @i{ana balṭi nissata īzib}; VII 86-7: [@i{ša i}]@i{qbû ul itūr ul i}[@i{pšiṭ}] / … @i{iddû ul itūr ul ipšiṭ} X 318 var.: @i{lullâ mītu ul ikruba} (@i{karābi}) @i{ina māti}.” See also @bib{RN602@408}: “Das gnomische Präteritum wurde auch in @i{Zahl und Zeit} S. 145-147 § 31 behandelt; dort sind auch weitere Belege aus dem Akkadischen genannt.”
